From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 09:20:26 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] sdk: lay out every interface's overloads and destructor as
 MSVC does

A game is built with MSVC and calls the emulator's interfaces through
MSVC's vtables. MSVC groups a class's overloaded virtuals at the first
one's slot and reverses them, and gives a virtual destructor one slot;
a MinGW build lays them out in declaration order and gives a destructor
two. So a MinGW build's ISteamUserStats::GetStat(int32 *) sat in the
game's GetStat(float *) slot (SetStat, GetUserStat, GetGlobalStat,
GetGlobalStatHistory and GetAchievementProgressLimits likewise), the
same in ISteamUGC, ISteamInventory, ISteamGameServerStats,
ISteamGameServerItems and two old interfaces, and every ISteamHTMLSurface
method was one slot off (the game's Init reached the deleting
destructor). On x86-64 and i386 alike.

A MinGW build now declares each such overload group in MSVC's order,
and a one-slot stand-in for the destructor (the emulator never deletes
through these interfaces). gbe 0004 did this for CCallbackBase's Run.
build/steamapi-vtables.py compares every interface's MinGW layout with
clang's MSVC layout before the build.

Class: upstream-bug
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-04-portal2-d3d9-vulkan.md
Offered-upstream: no
